WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.024
The settlement dashboard shows each merchant's payments today: how many and how much.

2
00:00:08.024 --> 00:00:11.844
A materialized view stores that answer, one row per merchant.

3
00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:15.689
The simple way to keep it right is to run the query again.

4
00:00:15.689 --> 00:00:22.180
A refresh reads all million payments and rewrites twenty-four thousand view rows, to change one number.

5
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:27.387
Refresh once a night, and a payment that arrives now is missing until tomorrow.

6
00:00:27.567 --> 00:00:34.656
Refresh every minute instead, and the database reads the whole table 1,440 times a day.

7
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:39.491
Incremental maintenance works from the change instead.

8
00:00:39.491 --> 00:00:42.768
Merchant 42 gets a new payment of 150.

9
00:00:42.768 --> 00:00:50.789
Its count goes up by one, its sum by 150, and its minimum becomes the smaller of 300 and 150.

10
00:00:50.933 --> 00:00:53.270
No payment was read from the table.

11
00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:56.408
One view row changed, however big the table is.

12
00:00:56.633 --> 00:00:59.141
A delete is the mirror image.

13
00:00:59.141 --> 00:01:03.896
Remove the 8,900: count down by one, sum down by 8,900.

14
00:01:03.896 --> 00:01:07.960
It was above the minimum, so the minimum stays.

15
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:10.760
Now delete the 150, the minimum itself.

16
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.444
Count and sum still work.

17
00:01:12.444 --> 00:01:17.967
But the view kept one number per merchant, and the next smallest was never stored.

18
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:20.695
So the delete goes back to the table.

19
00:01:20.695 --> 00:01:27.119
Through an index on the merchant, it reads merchant 42's two remaining payments and finds 300.

20
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.201
That costs one merchant's rows, not the table.

21
00:01:30.201 --> 00:01:35.057
The other way out is to keep every amount, sorted, and pay in memory instead.

22
00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:42.099
In the lab, a thousand changes to twenty thousand payments cost 1,402 rows of work this way.

23
00:01:42.099 --> 00:01:46.480
A full refresh after each change would read twenty million.

24
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.325
Count and sum follow from the change alone.

25
00:01:49.325 --> 00:01:53.903
A minimum needs the next value, so deleting it sends you back to the table.
